   "parts": [
    [
     1,
     "<div dir=\"ltr\">I&#39;ve got a model running that involves a truncated (gamma) distribution. I&#39;d like to implement a posterior predictive check and that means simulating replicated data which respects these truncation limits. Is there an easy and efficient way to implement this? <div><br></div><div>My thought was to use a while statement to reject any samples outside of the truncation limit, but I&#39;m not sure of how efficient this would be or whether this might introduce some bias in my replicated data.</div><div><br></div><div>For example one could try something like this:</div><div><br></div><div><div style=\"background-color:rgb(250,250,250);border-color:rgb(187,187,187);border-style:solid;border-width:1px;word-wrap:break-word\"><code><div><span style=\"color:#000\">generated quantities</span><span style=\"color:#660\">{</span><span style=\"color:#000\"><br>  real</span><span style=\"color:#660\">&lt;</span><span style=\"color:#000\">lower </span><span style=\"color:#660\">=</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> </span><span style=\"color:#066\">0</span><span style=\"color:#660\">&gt;</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> y_rep</span><span style=\"color:#660\">[</span><span style=\"color:#000\">N</span><span style=\"color:#660\">];</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> </span><span style=\"color:#800\">//gamma distributed random variable with upper truncation limit U</span><span style=\"color:#000\"><br><br>  </span><span style=\"color:#008\">for</span><span style=\"color:#660\">(</span><span style=\"color:#000\">n </span><span style=\"color:#008\">in</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> </span><span style=\"color:#066\">1</span><span style=\"color:#660\">:</span><span style=\"color:#000\">N</span><span style=\"color:#660\">){</span><span style=\"color:#000\"><br>    </span><span style=\"color:#008\">int</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> indic</span><span style=\"color:#660\">;</span><span style=\"color:#000\"><br>    real new_obs</span><span style=\"color:#660\">;</span><span style=\"color:#000\"><br><br>    indic </span><span style=\"color:#660\">=</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> </span><span style=\"color:#066\">0</span><span style=\"color:#660\">;</span><span style=\"color:#000\"><br>    </span><span style=\"color:#008\">while</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> </span><span style=\"color:#660\">(</span><span style=\"color:#000\">indic </span><span style=\"color:#660\">=</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> </span><span style=\"color:#066\">0</span><font color=\"#666600\"><span style=\"color:#660\">){</span><span style=\"color:#000\"><br>      new_obs </span><span style=\"color:#660\">=</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> gamma_rng</span><span style=\"color:#660\">(</span><span style=\"color:#000\">alpha</span><span style=\"color:#660\">,</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> beta</span><span style=\"color:#660\">);</span><span style=\"color:#000\"><br>      </span><span style=\"color:#008\">if</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> </span><span style=\"color:#660\">(</span><span style=\"color:#000\">new_obs </span><span style=\"color:#660\">&lt;</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> U</span><span style=\"color:#660\">)</span><span style=\"color:#000\"><br>        indic </span><span style=\"color:#660\">=</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> </span><span style=\"color:#066\">1</span><span style=\"color:#660\">;</span><span style=\"color:#000\"><br>    </span><span style=\"color:#660\">}</span><span style=\"color:#000\"><br></span></font><span style=\"color:#000\">    y_rep</span><span style=\"color:#660\">[</span><span style=\"color:#000\">n</span><span style=\"color:#660\">]</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> </span><span style=\"color:#660\">=</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> new_obs</span><span style=\"color:#660\">;</span><span style=\"color:#000\"><br>  </span><span style=\"color:#660\">}</span><span style=\"color:#000\"><br></span><span style=\"color:#660\">}</span></div></code></div><br>Are there problems with this approach? Is there a better way?</div></div>"
    ]
   ]
  },
